ManSection.Description:
    Numbers which are the order of two distinct simple groups occur twice.
    Default values for start and finish are 1 and
    infinity, respectively. If the option nopsl2 is set,
    numbers which occur only as orders of groups of type {\rm PSL}_2(q)
    are omitted. If the option names is set, the iterator produces not
    only the group orders, but rather it gives for each group a list which
    contains (in that ordering) the order, the name of the series, the
    parameters and a common name.


gap> iter := SimpleGroupsSizesIterator();                      
<iterator of orders of simple groups>
gap> NextIterator(iter);
60
gap> NextIterator(iter);
168
gap> NextIterator(iter);
360
gap> iter := SimpleGroupsSizesIterator(1:names);;
gap> NextIterator(iter);
[ 60, "Alt", [ 5 ], "A5" ]
gap> NextIterator(iter);
[ 168, "A", [ 2, 2 ], "PSL(3,2)" ]
gap> NextIterator(iter);
[ 360, "Alt", [ 6 ], "A6" ]
gap> NextIterator(iter);
[ 504, "A", [ 1, 8 ], "PSL(2,8)" ]
gap> NextIterator(iter);
[ 660, "A", [ 1, 11 ], "PSL(2,11)" ]
gap> iter := SimpleGroupsSizesIterator(1:nopsl2,names);;
gap> NextIterator(iter);
[ 60, "Alt", [ 5 ], "A5" ]
gap> NextIterator(iter);
[ 168, "A", [ 2, 2 ], "PSL(3,2)" ]
gap> NextIterator(iter);
[ 360, "Alt", [ 6 ], "A6" ]
gap> NextIterator(iter);
[ 2520, "Alt", [ 7 ], "A7" ]
gap> NextIterator(iter);
[ 5616, "A", [ 2, 3 ], "PSL(3,3)" ]
gap> iter := SimpleGroupsSizesIterator(1,10^20:names);
<iterator of orders of simple groups>
gap> L := [];;
gap> for G in iter do Add(L,G); od; 
gap> Length(L);
403874
gap> Collected(List(L,l->l[2]));
[ [ "2A", 106 ], [ "2B2", 6 ], [ "2D", 6 ], [ "2G2", 2 ], [ "3D4", 4 ], 
  [ "A", 403638 ], [ "Alt", 17 ], [ "B", 46 ], [ "C", 5 ], [ "D", 6 ], 
  [ "F4", 1 ], [ "G2", 13 ], [ "Spor", 24 ] ]
gap> Filtered([1..Length(L)],i->L[i][2]="3D4"); 
[ 194, 3875, 42970, 293920 ]
gap> L{last};
[ [ 211341312, "3D4", [ 2 ], "3D(4,2)" ], 
  [ 20560831566912, "3D4", [ 3 ], "3D(4,3)" ], 
  [ 67802350642790400, "3D4", [ 4 ], "3D(4,4)" ], 
  [ 35817806390625000000, "3D4", [ 5 ], "3D(4,5)" ] ]
gap> Filtered([1..Length(L)],i->L[i][2]="Spor");
[ 15, 31, 36, 46, 50, 98, 111, 138, 143, 203, 269, 388, 976, 1325, 1334, 
  1364, 4771, 5396, 8240, 39569, 46944, 150537, 151306, 386553 ]
gap> iter := SimpleGroupsSizesIterator(1,SizeSpor[27][2]+1:names,nopsl2);
<iterator of orders of simple groups>
gap> L := [];;
gap> for G in iter do Add(L,G); od;
gap> Length(L);
837104
gap> L[837104];           
[ 808017424794512875886459904961710757005754368000000000, "Spor", 
  [ 27 ], "M" ]

Chapter.Section:
Counting the simple groups of order up to a given bound

The following functions make use of the function 
,
and therefore require the program
primecount  by Kim Walisch
to be installed on the system:


  InfoSimpleGroupsSizes





    the number of non-abelian simple groups of order less than or equal
    to n.


    Set the Info level of the Info class InfoSimpleGroupsSizes
    to a value greater than 0 in order to get intermediate information
    on the progress from time to time when running a longer computation.


gap> NumberOfSimpleGroupsOfSizeAtMost(10^10);
493
gap> NumberOfSimpleGroupsOfSizeAtMost(10^20);
403874
gap> NumberOfSimpleGroupsOfSizeAtMost(10^30);
567362181
gap> NumberOfSimpleGroupsOfSizeAtMost(10^40);
907948153951
gap> NumberOfSimpleGroupsOfSizeAtMost(10^50);
1556185805218446








    the position of the non-abelian simple group G in the list
    of non-abelian simple groups sorted by increasing order.


    If there are two simple groups of the same order, it is not specified
    which of the two possible positions is returned. 


gap> PositionInListOfSimpleGroupsSortedBySize(AlternatingGroup(5));
1
gap> PositionInListOfSimpleGroupsSortedBySize(AlternatingGroup(30));
2695155612
gap> PositionInListOfSimpleGroupsSortedBySize(PSL(3,3));        
11
gap> PositionInListOfSimpleGroupsSortedBySize(PSL(5,5));
40693
gap> PositionInListOfSimpleGroupsSortedBySize(PSU(5,3));
1138
gap> PositionInListOfSimpleGroupsSortedBySize(Sz(8));   
23
gap> PositionInListOfSimpleGroupsSortedBySize(Sz(2^17));
22656043
gap> PositionInListOfSimpleGroupsSortedBySize(MathieuGroup(24));    
203
gap> PositionInListOfSimpleGroupsSortedBySize(SimpleGroup("HS"));
138
